WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680
Now that we've seen a real life example,

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:06.060
let's break down exactly how you assess someone

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.080
to see if they need your help.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.540
Think back to the video we just watched.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.220
What did you see the rescuer do first?

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:15.260
She was starting the four key steps

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:19.379
of assessment and activation that you see here on the screen.

00:00:19.379 --> 00:00:22.340
Step one is checking for responsiveness.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.580
To do this correctly, you want to tap the person firmly

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:29.820
on both shoulders and ask loudly, are you okay?

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.000
When we say someone is responsive,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.860
we mean they move, speak, blink,

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.040
or give you some kind of reaction.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.599
If they show any of these signs of life,

00:00:39.599 --> 00:00:42.259
they are alive and do not need CPR,

00:00:42.259 --> 00:00:44.700
but they might still need medical attention

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.880
through a call to 911.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.259
If you tap and shout and get no response at all,

00:00:50.259 --> 00:00:53.660
that is your signal that something is very wrong.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.099
As your instructor models this for you now,

00:00:56.099 --> 00:00:57.900
notice the firmness of the tap

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.700
and the clarity of the shout.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.860
Once you have determined that the person is not responding,

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:05.519
it is time for step two.

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:09.820
Shout for help, call 911, and get an AED.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.419
If there are people around you,

00:01:11.419 --> 00:01:14.080
it is very important to avoid generic statements

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.739
like someone call 911.

00:01:16.739 --> 00:01:18.260
In a high stress moment,

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:21.720
people often need a direct command to take action.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.860
You should point to a specific person

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.120
and tell them to call 911,

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.559
then point to another specific person

00:01:28.559 --> 00:01:30.959
and tell them to find an AED.

00:01:30.959 --> 00:01:33.879
Now, what if you ask a stranger to get an AED

00:01:33.879 --> 00:01:35.919
and they do not know what it is?

00:01:35.919 --> 00:01:38.919
Just tell them to ask around or look for one,

00:01:38.919 --> 00:01:42.459
and if they find it, to bring it back to you.

00:01:42.459 --> 00:01:45.599
They might not find one, but delegating that task

00:01:45.599 --> 00:01:48.680
allows you to stay focused on the person in need.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.680
If you are completely by yourself,

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.519
call 911 on your own phone immediately

00:01:53.519 --> 00:01:55.320
and put it on speakerphone.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.440
This is essential because it allows you to begin CPR

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.139
while following the dispatcher's instructions.

00:02:01.139 --> 00:02:03.320
You might wonder if you should leave the person

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.320
to go find an AED.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.800
You should only leave to retrieve an AED

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.639
if you know exactly where it is

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:12.039
and can get back very quickly.

00:02:12.039 --> 00:02:13.699
Never go on a search mission

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:16.080
and leave the person unattended.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.800
Your main priority is to stay right there and provide care.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.839
Now we move to step three,

00:02:22.839 --> 00:02:24.320
which is checking for breathing

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:29.320
by using a simple method, look, listen, and feel.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:31.899
Start by looking closely at the person's chest

00:02:31.899 --> 00:02:34.119
to see if it is rising and falling.

00:02:34.119 --> 00:02:36.720
At the same time, place your ear and cheek

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.979
near their mouth and nose so you can listen

00:02:38.979 --> 00:02:41.279
and feel for any air moving.

00:02:41.279 --> 00:02:43.619
Now, this part is critical.

00:02:43.619 --> 00:02:45.639
If the person is only gasping,

00:02:45.639 --> 00:02:47.759
that is not normal breathing.

00:02:47.759 --> 00:02:49.720
We call this agonal breathing,

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.440
and it often sounds like weak,

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.059
irregular snorting or gasping.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:55.580
If you see or hear this,

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.740
do not mistake it for normal breathing.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.479
You must begin CPR immediately.

00:03:00.479 --> 00:03:01.820
If they are breathing normally,

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.660
they do not need CPR.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.100
In that case, you should wait for help to arrive,

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.779
and you can gently roll them onto their left side

00:03:08.779 --> 00:03:10.779
into the recovery position.

00:03:10.779 --> 00:03:13.179
As we practice this on our mannequins right now,

00:03:13.179 --> 00:03:15.179
I want you to really get down there.

00:03:15.179 --> 00:03:18.339
Lean in close, put your ear to the mannequin's mouth,

00:03:18.339 --> 00:03:20.259
and keep your eyes on the chest

00:03:20.259 --> 00:03:22.979
so you can feel for that rise and fall.

00:03:22.979 --> 00:03:25.419
You might have heard about checking for a pulse,

00:03:25.419 --> 00:03:28.580
but for lay rescuers, we do not advise this

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:30.740
as it can be difficult to do correctly

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:34.660
in a high-stress moment and uses up valuable time.

00:03:35.539 --> 00:03:37.860
Now we have reached step four,

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.179
the most critical part of our rescue,

00:03:40.179 --> 00:03:42.300
beginning chest compressions.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:44.580
By now, you have determined the person

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.979
is both unresponsive and not breathing normally.

00:03:47.979 --> 00:03:50.899
That is your absolute green light to act.

00:03:50.899 --> 00:03:53.539
If they aren't responding and they aren't breathing,

00:03:53.539 --> 00:03:55.100
their heart is not pumping,

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:58.300
and they need you to do that job for them manually.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.820
Think of chest compressions

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.899
as manually pumping the heart from the outside.

00:04:02.899 --> 00:04:05.300
It is the act of rhythmically pressing down

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:06.979
on the center of the chest

00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:09.500
to circulate blood to the brain and lungs.

